thread:
maria: hi all, running into a Next.js middleware import failure when using our JS SDK in the edge runtime. error is thrown at import time: "Reference to Node builtin 'fs' not allowed in Edge". any patterns to make the SDK edge-friendly without splitting the repo into two packages?

oliver: yeah this is common. Next Edge will eagerly evaluate imports and will bail if any module graph references Node builtins. the usual approach is an explicit small edge entrypoint (subpath) that only pulls runtime-safe code.

jess: we shipped a pattern in an example app: create dist/edge.mjs that only exports the minimal client surface you need in middleware, then expose it at an export subpath. users import from `@our/sdk/edge` in middleware.

maria: can you show the package.json changes?

jess: sure, minimal example:

```json
{
  "name": "@our/sdk",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    },
    "./edge": {
      "import": "./dist/edge.mjs"
    }
  },
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts",
  "typesVersions": {
    "*": {
      "./edge": ["dist/edge.d.ts"]
    }
  }
}
```

jess: key is the explicit `./edge` subpath. avoid inventing conditional keys like "edge" at the top level; tools don't consistently support it.

kevin: build side: produce an edge bundle that targets modern browsers and does not pull in node libs. esbuild flags we used: `platform=browser`, `format=esm`, `target=es2020`, and `external: ['node:crypto', 'fs']` to ensure tree-shaking removes them.

sam: watch for transitive deps. Even if your code doesn't import fs, a telemetry or http client dep might. you have to either replace that dep with a thin edge-safe alternative in the edge build or stub it out with a tiny shim that throws on unsupported APIs.

maria: what about types? typesVersions above will map `import from '@our/sdk/edge'` to dist/edge.d.ts? does tsserver pick that up?

jess: yeah, typesVersions works in tsc/VSCode for path mapping of subpath imports. alternatively, ship a dist/edge.d.ts and also reference it from exports with "types": "dist/edge.d.ts" under `./edge` if you use npm >= 13 metadata for exports types (but typesVersions is broader for older toolchains).

kevin: small gotcha: bundlers sometimes rewrite `@our/sdk` to the main export if using an import like `import sdk from '@our/sdk'` somewhere else in the app. be explicit in middleware imports: `import { createClient } from '@our/sdk/edge'` so the resolver picks the subpath.

maria: CI found this only after deploying to staging and the middleware crashed. any way to detect this locally?

oliver: run `node --test` doesn't simulate Edge. we use `esbuild --platform=browser` to build the edge artifact and then run a small local worker using Miniflare or Cloudflare Workers runtime to smoke test. also Next has `next dev` but it can still resolve wrong entry in monorepos.

jess: we also added a lint rule in our SDK repo that flags any new import of node builtins from the public entrypoints. prevented regressions that way.

sam: for DX: add a clear README snippet under "Using in Next.js middleware" showing `import { createClient } from '@our/sdk/edge'` and a tiny example middleware. that saves onboarding trouble.

maria: thanks everyone. I'll try building `dist/edge.mjs` with esbuild and wire up typesVersions. will post CI logs if it still fails. :pray:

